{
 "indicator": {
  "definition": {
   "definition": "The money multiplier is the ratio of broad money to reserve money — how many rupees of M3 the banking system creates per rupee of base money — computed here as M3 divided by reserve money on the nearest matching weekly observation. RBI publishes both components (M3 in 'Money Stock: Components and Sources', reserve money in 'Reserve Money: Components and Sources') but does not publish this ratio as a series, so the figure shown is derived by this dashboard. A rising multiplier means banks are converting base money into deposits and credit more efficiently; a falling multiplier means liquidity is being absorbed into reserves that are not being lent on.",
   "source_name": "Derived in this dashboard from RBI WSS 'Money Stock' (M3) and 'Reserve Money' — components published by RBI, ratio is not an RBI series",
   "source_url": "https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/PublicationsView.aspx?id=17618",
